            0.587,
            0.882,
            0.683
          ],
          "content": "3. You know that the sum of angles of a quadrilateral is $3 6 0 ^ { \\circ }$ , even for a non-convex quadrilateral. (Recall the proof.) Now consider a self-intersecting quadrilateral ABCD, where AB and CD intersect at point E. Show that $\\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C + \\angle D < 360^{\\circ}$ . Can you construct ABCD such that $\\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C + \\angle D = 2^{\\circ}?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.688,
            0.882,
            0.748
          ],
          "content": "4. In a parallelogram ABCD, two points P and Q are taken on diagonal BD such that DP = BQ (see Fig. 12.34). Show that APCQ is a parallelogram."
